git rev-parse --parseopt keeps short options in their short form in
the generated set -- output (it emits -c, not --commits). The while
loop matches both -c and --commits to stay correct across git
versions that might normalize differently.

The test harness needs 8 unchanged context lines between the two
edited lines to force two distinct conflict hunks. With fewer lines,
git's merge driver may collapse both conflicts into one region. This
is a scaffolding detail, not a script limitation.

I considered matching ======= markers as well as <<<<<<< and >>>>>>>
to split the hunk at the ours/theirs boundary, but left it out — the
combined view with both sides in one blame pass is more useful, and
the zero-SHA markers already delimit the sides visually.
